      "claim": "When the object is at infinity (d_o → ∞), the image distance d_i equals the focal length f.",
      "evidence": "Applying the mirror equation: as 1/d_o → 0, 1/d_i = 1/f, so d_i = f.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "As the object moves from infinity toward the focal point (d_o decreases but remains > f), the image distance d_i increases.",
      "evidence": "From 1/d_i = 1/f - 1/d_o, as d_o decreases, 1/d_o increases, making the right-hand side smaller. A smaller positive value for 1/d_i implies a larger d_i.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [
        1,
        2
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "An increase in image distance d_i means the image moves away from the mirror.",
      "evidence": "Image distance is measured from the mirror's vertex; a larger positive d_i corresponds to a position further from the mirror.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [
        3
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The magnitude of magnification |m| = d_i/d_o increases as the object approaches the focal point.",
      "evidence": "Substituting d_i = f*d_o/(d_o - f) into m = -d_i/d_o gives |m| = f/(d_o - f). As d_o decreases toward f, the denominator approaches 0, causing |m| to increase toward infinity.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [
        1,
        3
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "An increase in magnification means the image gets taller.",
      "evidence": "Magnification is the ratio of image height to object height; if |m| increases, the image height increases relative to the object height.",
